{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central diagnostic criterion fails its own internal logic: it claims that clustered panel errors on cases where the checklist is silent indicate mere compliance with an incomplete checklist, but its own countercondition (\"the expert's intuition is also anchored to the same incomplete set of features\") shows that error clustering on unchecklisted features cannot distinguish a checklist-specific compliance failure from a generic feature-omission failure shared by both systems. If the expert also fails those cases, the checklist is not causally incomplete in a way that privileges expert intuition—yet the claim treats such clustering as evidence that \"the reliability gain is merely compliance with an incomplete proxy,\" presupposing the expert would succeed. The transferred diagnostic rule is therefore question-begging: it assumes the expert uses additional causally relevant features (the very point at issue) to license the inference from error clustering to \"mere compliance.\"","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist and expert both omit feature F. Panel errors cluster on F-cases; expert errors also cluster on F-cases. By SRC_001's rule these would be flagged as 'mere compliance,' but no incomplete-proxy advantage of expert intuition exists, so the rule misclassifies a shared limitation as a checklist-specific defect."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's underdetermination thesis is internally inconsistent with its own counterconditions. It asserts that genuine reliability gain is \"fundamentally indistinguishable\" from mere compliance within a single evaluation because both produce identical observables, yet it lists as counterconditions process-tracing data and adversarial/OOD cases *within* a single evaluation that would distinguish them. If those counterconditions are genuine possibilities within a single evaluation (as the target concedes by listing them rather than ruling them out), then the claim that they are \"fundamentally indistinguishable within a single evaluation\" is overstated: the target itself admits single-evaluation designs that yield the distinguishing evidence. The argument thus collapses into the weaker claim that distinguishability depends on evaluation design, not that it is impossible within a single evaluation.","counterexample":[{"design":"A single evaluation dataset containing adversarial perturbation cases where the checklist's correlated proxy breaks down; panel accuracy collapses on those cases while the expert's does not—distinguishing mere compliance from genuine reliability within one evaluation, contrary to the 'fundamentally indistinguishable' claim."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}